<!-- Student-story narration source.
     One <speak> block per slide.
     Render via scripts/render_narration.sh (operator workflow).
     Voice: Polly Salli (neural). -->

<!-- slide-01.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Five layers, one lesson.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Text. <break time="180ms"/> Images. <break time="180ms"/> Animation. <break time="180ms"/> Quiz. <break time="180ms"/> Tutorial.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Built for the student who'll use it — not for everyone at once.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-02.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Meet Maya — Grade eleven Science.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Tonight she's working through Newton's laws on her phone.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Her lesson is already waiting — five layers, about twenty-five minutes of focused study.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-03.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Layer one — text.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Plain language. Pitched two grades below her own so it never feels like a wall of jargon.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Heavy terms get defined inline — inertia gets a one-line gloss the first time it appears.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-04.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Layer two — images.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Force diagrams. A position-time graph.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    And the hurricane evacuation map from this week's news,
    drawn for her grade level — connecting Newton's laws to something she actually saw on TV.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-05.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Layer three — animation.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    For motion topics, a short clip plays right inside the lesson.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Two motion strips: uniform motion with equal gaps, accelerated motion with growing gaps.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    No tab to chase. No video to find.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-06.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Layer four — quiz.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Eight questions. Maya gets one wrong on her first try.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    There's no red X. Instead — a friendly two-sentence explanation written for the misconception,
    not the topic.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-07.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Layer five — tutorial.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    When the quiz says she's stuck, the lesson opens up the steps.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Equation, substitution, solve. Every line shown. Every reason explained.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-08.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Five layers — one lesson.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Read. <break time="200ms"/> Look. <break time="200ms"/> Watch. <break time="200ms"/> Test. <break time="200ms"/> Fix.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    All in the same place — no twenty tabs, no guessing what to do next.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-09.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Twelve nights in a row.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    The streak counter knows.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    The encouragement nudge knows too — but it only fires when it's earned.
    No fake pep talks.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-10.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Maya has dyslexia — and accessibility is one toggle away.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Wider letter spacing. Broader line height. OpenDyslexic mode whenever she needs it.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    The setting follows her account — phone, laptop, library kiosk.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-11.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Maya gets Grade eleven Science.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Her cousin gets Grade eleven Commerce.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Her sister gets Grade eight STEM.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Same engine. Different lesson. Every time.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-12.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    StudyBuddy.
    <break time="200ms"/>
    Lessons, always current.
    <break time="600ms"/>
    Try it tonight at demo dot studybuddy dot app —
    or have your school reach out to support at studybuddy dot app.
  </prosody>
</speak>
